---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { ISSUES_URL, REPO_LINKS_ENABLED, REPO_URL } from '../site';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

// The app facts on this page were corrected by the security review (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW.md, F-14)
// and checked against src-tauri/tauri.conf.json (bundle identifier dev.krova.desktop). Only the
// "This website" section and one sentence about the source code changed when the site was redesigned.
const title = 'Privacy: Krova';
const description =
  'Krova makes no network calls, has no accounts or telemetry, and never logs window titles. Here is exactly what it stores on your computer, and where.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy, in plain language</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Krova is an offline app. It does not send anything anywhere, so there is no data of yours for us to hold,
        sell or lose.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose" aria-label="Privacy details">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Krova makes <strong>no network calls</strong>.</li>
        <li>There are <strong>no accounts</strong> and <strong>no telemetry</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Window titles are never logged or stored.</strong></li>
        <li>Your settings and layouts stay in <strong>one local JSON file</strong> that you can read, copy or delete.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Krova does on the network</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing. The app makes no network calls at all: no sign-in, no usage statistics, no crash reports, no update
        pings. Because it never connects out, there is no server on the other side that could receive your data.
      </p>
      <p>
        You do not have to take our word for it. {
          REPO_LINKS_ENABLED ? (
            <Fragment>The source code is open under the MIT License, and you</Fragment>
          ) : (
            <Fragment>You</Fragment>
          )
        } can watch Krova with your operating system&rsquo;s network monitor or block it in your firewall. It should behave
        exactly the same.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things sit outside Krova&rsquo;s own code. The Windows installer downloads Microsoft&rsquo;s WebView2 runtime if it is
        not already installed on your computer. And the web view that draws Krova&rsquo;s window (WebView2 on Windows,
        WebKit on macOS) and the operating system&rsquo;s own crash reporting are separate system components that Krova does
        not control.
      </p>

      <h2>What is stored on your computer</h2>
      <p>
        Krova keeps your settings and layouts in one file, <code>config.json</code>, in your user account&rsquo;s
        application-data folder (typically <code>%APPDATA%\dev.krova.desktop</code> on Windows and
        <code>~/Library/Application Support/dev.krova.desktop</code> on macOS). It contains:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Settings:</strong> the activation key, highlight color and opacity, animated or instant moves, edge
          snapping on or off and its size, launch at login, enabled or disabled, and theme.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Per-monitor data:</strong> for each monitor, an identifier, a label, its layouts (zones saved as
          proportions of the screen) and which layout is active.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To recognise a monitor when you plug it back in, Krova stores what the operating system reports about it: the
        manufacturer and product codes, the device path (which includes an identifier for the port it is plugged
        into), and the monitor&rsquo;s last size and position. On macOS this also includes the display&rsquo;s serial number
        where the display reports one; on Windows Krova does not store a serial number. These are what let Krova bring
        a layout back for the same monitor. They stay in this file on your machine. If you ever share your
        <code>config.json</code>, for example in a bug report, remove those identifiers first if you would rather not
        share them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The file is written safely (to a temporary file, then renamed), so a temporary <code>config.json.tmp</code>
        can exist for a moment. If the file ever turns out to be unreadable, Krova renames it to
        <code>config.json.bak</code> and starts again with defaults instead of discarding it.
      </p>
      <p>Besides <code>config.json</code>, a few other things exist on your computer while Krova is installed:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          On Windows, the web view that draws Krova&rsquo;s window keeps its own cache and browser profile in a folder under
          <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\dev.krova.desktop</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          If you turn on &ldquo;Launch at login&rdquo;, Krova adds a start-up entry: a value named &ldquo;Krova&rdquo; under
          <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</code> on Windows, or a
          <code>dev.krova.desktop.plist</code> file in <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents</code> on macOS when it is not run from
          an app bundle (otherwise a regular macOS login item).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Krova does not record</h2>
      <p>
        To notice that you are dragging a window, Krova listens to mouse movement and clicks across your whole
        computer while it runs. When you press the mouse button on a window it looks at that window&rsquo;s position and size
        and whether its program runs as administrator, and during a drag it checks whether your activation key and
        Escape are held. That all happens in memory, in the moment, and none of it is stored or sent anywhere.
        <strong>Window titles are never read, logged or stored</strong>, and nothing about the windows you move is
        written to <code>config.json</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        On macOS, Krova asks for <strong>Accessibility</strong> permission (System Settings, Privacy &amp; Security,
        Accessibility). macOS requires it before any app can move and resize other apps&rsquo; windows. Depending on your
        macOS version it may also ask for <strong>Input Monitoring</strong> so that it can notice a window being
        dragged. Krova only watches the left mouse button and checks whether your activation key and Escape are held;
        it does not read or record what you type. On Windows no special permission is requested; windows of apps
        running as administrator are skipped.
      </p>

      <h2>Removing your data</h2>
      <p>
        Delete <code>config.json</code> (and <code>config.json.bak</code>, if present) to erase the settings and
        layouts Krova has stored. To remove everything else, turn off &ldquo;Launch at login&rdquo; before uninstalling and delete
        the <code>dev.krova.desktop</code> folder under <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%</code> on Windows. Krova keeps no copy of your
        data anywhere else, because it never sends anything anywhere. The
        <a href={url('/guides/install/#uninstall')}>install guide</a> has the full list and the steps.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site is a set of static pages. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics, has no forms and loads nothing from
        other servers: no fonts, images, scripts or embeds. It runs one small script of its own, a file served from this
        site, that remembers your light or dark choice in your browser&rsquo;s local storage (the key
        <code>krova-theme</code>). Nothing is sent anywhere. Like any web server, whatever hosts these pages may keep
        ordinary access logs; the site itself does nothing to add to that.
      </p>
      <p>
        The live editor on the home page is also part of this site. It runs in your browser against sample monitors and
        keeps the layouts you edit in your browser&rsquo;s local storage (the key <code>krova-demo</code>). It makes no
        network requests and cannot touch your windows.
      </p>
      <p>
        Downloading an installer necessarily contacts the server that hosts it (for example GitHub, if you get it from
        the releases page). That request is handled under that service&rsquo;s own privacy policy, not by Krova.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        {
          REPO_LINKS_ENABLED ? (
            <Fragment>
              If something here is unclear or looks wrong, please <a href={ISSUES_URL}>open an issue</a> in the{' '}
              <a href={REPO_URL}>project repository</a>.
            </Fragment>
          ) : (
            <Fragment>
              If something here is unclear or looks wrong, please open an issue in the project repository once it is
              public. It is not public yet.
            </Fragment>
          )
        }
      </p>
      <p class="muted">Last updated: 30 October 2026. See also <a href={url('/license/')}>License and terms</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  article {
    padding-bottom: var(--section-y);
  }
  article :global(h2:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
